\subsection{Computational Cost}
\label{app:computational-cost}

Table~\ref{tab:judge-cost} summarizes the nine production judge runs on the full 300-record pool. The 2,688 logged calls produced 2,683 verdicts and five failures. Input/output columns report recorded token counts; call time sums all event latencies (including failures), rather than elapsed wall-clock time.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\scriptsize
\begin{tabular*}{\linewidth}{@{\extracolsep{\fill}}lrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Judge & Calls & Verdicts & Input & Output & Hours \\
\midrule
GPT & 896 & 892 & 1,854,434 & 106,135 & 0.628 \\
Grok & 896 & 895 & 1,842,371 & 105,026 & 2.833 \\
Gemini & 896 & 896 & 1,930,153 & 97,020 & 1.422 \\
\midrule
Total & 2,688 & 2,683 & 5,626,958 & 308,181 & 4.883 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular*}
\caption{Recorded usage for the full judge matrix.}
\label{tab:judge-cost}
\end{table}

These totals cover judging only. Generation and one adoption-labeling pass each scale as $NK$; benchmark construction is additional. Calls are application-level events, so provider-internal retries are not separately counted. Token totals exclude unrecorded failed-call and retry usage and Gemini thinking tokens. The totals are reproduced by \texttt{audit/judge\_cost.py}.
